"""The PRISM Analysis of every driver, by driver id.

Generated by scripts/generate_seed.py from the texts in
data/drivers/driver_analysis.json, which scripts/build_driver_analysis.py
renders from the attribution record. Do not edit this file. The texts are
read-only: the API serves each with its stored driver, and no database
holds them (docs/DRIVER_ANALYSIS.md).
"""

TEXTS = {
    "competitive_r15": "Weight in the answer: This driver carries 0.90 of the 5.6 points of net exposure at 2035, the largest single contribution of the 51 and 72 percent of the Competitive force's headwind. The size comes from breadth rather than from its expected effect, which C-01 exceeds. It reaches 71 percent of the pool, more than any other driver, because it touches every category and is strongest in the two regions that together hold 75 percent of the estimated mix, and in a force of four drivers the overlap correction costs it only 4 percent, a fraction of what the Customer and Technology drivers lose to it.\n\nWhere it lands: Its two largest cells are the largest and the third largest cells of the mix, 10.8 and 9.2 percent of the pool: fabric clean Europe (0.14 points, 14 percent of that cell's shift) and fabric clean North America (0.12 points, 21 percent). In hair it is a larger share of a smaller pool: 23 percent of the hair care contraction, 20 percent of hair colour and 18 percent of hair styling, so the hair categories are where a reader meets it first. Europe and North America hold 82 percent of the effect; Asia, where it is nearly as present, holds 5 percent because the region is a twentieth of the pool. It shares the laundry cells with X-17 (share windows from incumbent retrenchment) and the hair cells with X-16 (challenger fragmentation) and X-18 (the professional channel), and the overlap correction already discounts the four together.\n\nWhen it lands: A steady escalation, not a shock: 44 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 74 percent by 2030, the half-way point falls in 2029 and the effect is complete by 2034. Nothing in it is dated; it accrues a little more in every year of the horizon.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own P10 to P90 range runs from 0.56 to 1.20 points, the widest of any driver, and it accounts for 31 percent of the portfolio band's variance, about a third of the uncertainty in the headline. The width is magnitude uncertainty: the size of the leaders' combined innovation and media effect on HCB is set by judgment and measured by no series, so the largest item in the answer is also the least evidenced of the five largest, and the share effect on HCB is contested, since Henkel gained European fabric-care share in H1 2026 while P&G reported its weakest year since 2019. The observables are the leaders' advertising ratios (L'Oréal 32.6 percent of sales in H1 2026, Unilever 16.1 percent of turnover), P&G's unit-dose share in fabric care and Henkel's own European share prints: two consecutive half-years of HCB share gain against the leaders in hair or laundry would argue for a smaller magnitude, a widening media gap for a larger one.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "competitive_r16": "Weight in the answer: Challenger fragmentation in hair carries 0.19 of the 5.6 points, the twelfth largest contribution of the 51 and 15 percent of the Competitive headwind. A hair-only driver can reach at most the 45 percent of the pool that the hair categories hold; this one reaches 25 percent, because it is concentrated in hair care, the second-largest hair category, and less present in colour, the largest; the Competitive pass-through (8.0 percent) and the light overlap correction of a four-driver force (6 percent) are what keep it in the top dozen.\n\nWhere it lands: Hair care Europe (0.03 points, 9 percent of that cell's shift) and hair colour Europe (0.03 points) carry 36 percent of the effect; Europe holds 52 percent and North America 27 percent, where TikTok Shop and Amazon incubate the challengers, while Asia, the home of the K-beauty and C-beauty brands, holds 9 percent because the region is a twentieth of the pool. It accounts for 11 percent of the hair care contraction, 9 percent of hair styling and 8 percent of hair colour. It shares every hair cell with X-15 (scale-leader escalation) and X-18 (the professional channel), and with K-04 (social commerce) in the Customer force, which carries the channel-margin half of the same story: the two should be read as one social-commerce complex, capture here, channel terms there.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2031: 53 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 83 percent by 2029, the half-way point is 2028 and the effect is complete by 2031. The early years are slow because the fragmentation that is measured is the American one, and the hair extension of K-beauty, which NIQ names as the next phase, has not yet shown in the European series.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.12 to 0.25 points and it accounts for 4 percent of the portfolio band's variance. The measured half is the US fragmentation (USD 2.08bn of TikTok Shop US beauty sales in H1 2026, hair challengers above USD 85m); the uncertain half is whether K-beauty extends from skincare into hair, since Korean exports are 74.7 percent skincare. The observables are NIQ's K-beauty series for Western Europe (plus 58 percent), Amorepacific's overseas revenue and the trigger already written into the driver: Chinese hair brands reaching 3 percent of any EU online hair market, which the EUR 3 per-item parcel duty of 1 July 2026 has made less likely, not more.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "competitive_r17": "Weight in the answer: Share windows opened by incumbent retrenchment offset 0.07 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the ninth largest tailwind of the 18 and the only tailwind in the Competitive force. It reaches 24 percent of the pool, the categories where a window has actually opened, and its weight is held down by a small expected effect, which prices in that share does not transfer automatically: private label and the number-two brand take part of every window, Advent is investing in Vestacy and McBride keeps its cost base competitive. The Competitive pass-through is the second highest of the six forces (8.0 percent against the Consumer rate of 8.1), and the overlap correction of a four-driver force is the lightest in the base (3 percent), which is why a small driver still registers.\n\nWhere it lands: Europe holds 64 percent of the effect, High Growth 15 percent. The largest category shares are fabric care (5 percent of the contraction offset), insect control (4 percent) and hard-surface cleaner (4 percent): Woolite against Perwoll, Mortein against the insect-control range, Cillit Bang against Bref and WC Frisch. Auto dishwash carries none of it, because Finish stayed with Reckitt. It sits on the same cells as X-15 (scale-leader escalation), the force's large headwind, and the two are the two faces of competitive intensity: the leaders' escalation on one side, the retrenchment of the weaker incumbents on the other.\n\nWhen it lands: Linear to 2028 rather than front-loaded, because shelves cannot move before the 2026 resets and the capture arrives a third a year: 71 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027, 90 percent by 2028 and the effect is complete by 2030.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.04 to 0.11 points, from about half of the expected value to half again above it, because the capture could be zero or double. The series to watch are the Vestacy brands' like-for-like trajectory after the 31 December 2025 completion and the McBride production transfer in 2027, P&G's 30 to 50 basis points of FY2027 growth given up to exits, NIVEA's 18-month turnaround after minus 6.8 percent organic in H1 2026, and Coty's transition year. The first European shelf resets of 2026 and 2027 are where the window either transfers or closes; a window that private label takes would argue for the lower end of the range and, through C-01, for a larger private-label driver.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "competitive_r18": "Weight in the answer: Professional channel restructuring carries 0.16 of the 5.6 points, the 16th largest contribution of the 51 and 13 percent of the Competitive headwind. It is a colour-and-care driver of the salon channel, so it reaches 28 percent of the pool, and it keeps a place in the upper half of the ranking through the Competitive pass-through (8.0 percent) and the light overlap correction of its force (6 percent).\n\nWhere it lands: Hair colour Europe carries 34 percent of the effect (0.06 points, 10 percent of that cell's shift), because salon colour is the professional core; hair care Europe (0.03 points) is next. Outside Europe its weight is in the small North American and High Growth colour cells, where it accounts for 19 and 15 percent of the cell shift respectively, larger shares of smaller cells. It accounts for 12 percent of the hair colour contraction and 9 percent of hair care. It is the contraction side of a channel whose expansion side is K-07 (professional crossover into retail): the crossover lifts the price ladder, this driver carries the salon-volume erosion, distributor consolidation and diversion that come with it, and the two are one restructuring read from both ends.\n\nWhen it lands: A linear ramp to 2030: 64 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 93 percent by 2030, the half-way point is 2028 and the effect is complete by 2032.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.10 to 0.22 points and it accounts for 3 percent of the portfolio band's variance; salon volume erosion and pro-brand migration could be half or double the modelled size. The observables are the Kline channel series (e-commerce gained 15.6 points of hair-care sales over 2019 to 2024 while salon back bar and take-home lost 7.5 and 8.5 points), L'Oréal PPD's outside-salon share (39 percent) and its growth against the professional market, and the Wella listing announced in August 2026, which adds a well-funded competitor at about USD 2.9bn of revenue. The taxonomy has no professional category: the salon business sits inside the hair colour and care cells, so a driver specific to it reads smaller than its relevance to a business in which professional and prestige hair is roughly 15 percent of HCB sales pro forma after Olaplex.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r01": "Weight in the answer: Private label carries 0.76 of the 5.6 points, the second largest contribution of the 51 and 36 percent of the Consumer force's headwind, the largest item in that force. It has the largest expected effect in the base, and the mix is what holds it below X-15: the driver reaches 57 percent of the pool because own label barely exists in hair colour and styling and is marginal in Asia, so most of the hair pool and nearly all of the Asian pool sit outside its reach. The overlap correction costs it 8 percent, the ordinary Consumer rate.\n\nWhere it lands: Fabric clean Europe is its largest cell (0.15 points, 15 percent of that cell's shift), followed by fabric clean North America (0.07 points) and auto dishwash Europe (0.07 points, 17 percent of the cell). The effect is 68 percent European and 20 percent North American. Its weight is clearest in the small laundry and dish categories: it accounts for 18 percent of the fabric finishers contraction, 17 percent of laundry additives and 17 percent of auto dishwash, the categories where formulation parity is easiest and the shelf is retailer-controlled. In the European laundry cells it sits next to C-06 (the cost-of-living squeeze), the other large Consumer headwind, and the two should be read as one price-and-promotion complex rather than added: the same inflation that re-accelerates own label is what drives trade-down.\n\nWhen it lands: Front-loaded to 2030, because inflation years are when own label gains fastest: 64 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027, 78 percent by 2028 and 98 percent by 2030, with the half-way point in 2027. The driver counts only the incremental gain over the horizon, not the installed base of own-label share.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.62 to 0.88 points, comparatively narrow for its size, and it accounts for 9 percent of the portfolio band's variance. The direction is not in doubt; the width is the pace, since the annual own-label gain has ranged from minus 0.2 to plus 1.5 share points. The observables are the Circana and NIQ own-label series (EU6 unit share 50 percent, value share 42 percent in April 2026) and the German panel for laundry (about 20 percent of value) and cleaners (about 30 percent): a year in which national brands hold share through the energy shock would argue for a lower pace, a year of premium own-label growth above the branded tier, as NIQ measured in June 2026, for a higher one.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r02": "Weight in the answer: GLP-1 adoption offsets 0.04 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the 17th largest tailwind of the 18. It is a hair-care driver led by North America, and it reaches only 12 percent of the pool, because Europe is structurally lower rather than behind (about 2 percent of adults, reimbursement limited) and the grocery-spend effect of the drugs does not reach any HCB category; nothing in the pass-through or the overlap correction sets it apart from the other Consumer drivers.\n\nWhere it lands: The effect is split evenly between Europe (41 percent) and North America (41 percent) although the driver is far more present in North America, because Europe holds more than twice North America's share of the pool; in hair care North America the driver offsets 8 percent of the cell's net contraction, its largest share of any cell, while in the larger European cells it is a fraction of that. It offsets 3 percent of the hair care contraction and 2 percent of body care. It sits on the same cells as C-03 (hair-care premiumisation), C-10 (thinning) and C-07 (scalp care); the GLP-1 shedding occasion enters the numbers through this driver's magnitude while C-10 carries the thinning regimen that responds to it, and the four should be read as one science-led hair complex.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2031 as oral GLP-1s broaden adoption and then plateau: 53 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 96 percent by 2030, the half-way point is 2028 and the effect is complete by 2031. What would move the timing is the price and reimbursement path of the oral formulations: the curve assumes adoption keeps broadening to the end of the decade rather than saturating at today's 11 percent of US adults.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.02 to 0.05 points. Adoption is measured (11 percent of US adults, 22 to 23 percent of households with a user, oral Wegovy past 3 million prescriptions in five months) and the width is the HCB-relevant slice, which is hard to size and depends on oral adoption and price; the clinical base rate of GLP-1-associated hair loss is small even where the demand response is visible (US prestige hair serums up nearly 60 percent in H1 2026). What to watch is Gallup's adoption series, the JAAD cohort literature and the Circana and Numerator reallocation series (users cut grocery spend about 4 percent after a year and reallocate to self-care); a European adoption rate that starts to climb would be the one change that widens the driver's reach.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r03": "Weight in the answer: Hair-care premiumisation is the largest tailwind in the base: it offsets 0.36 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, 21 percent of the 1.7-point tailwind that the 18 expansion drivers add up to, and the eighth largest contribution of the 51 in absolute terms. Its expected effect is among the highest in the base, and it still reaches only 33 percent of the pool, because it is a hair-only driver: no laundry or home-care cell carries any of it, and those cells hold 55 percent of the estimated mix.\n\nWhere it lands: Hair colour Europe (0.08 points, offsetting 15 percent of that cell's net contraction) and hair care Europe (0.06 points, 16 percent) carry 40 percent of the effect; in hair colour Asia it offsets 32 percent of the cell's contraction, its largest share of any cell. It is the largest single reason the hair categories contract less than laundry: it offsets 20 percent of the hair care contraction, 18 percent of hair colour and 16 percent of hair styling. It sits on the same cells as C-35 (mass unit decline), its contraction counterpart, and the two are one value migration read from both ends; C-04 (clinical-efficacy premiumisation) and K-07 (professional crossover) carry adjacent mechanisms and should not be added to it as independent upside.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2030: 27 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027, 75 percent by 2028, 96 percent by 2029, and the effect is complete by 2030. The premium tier's growth differential is a measured series, so the curve carries no dated event.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.29 to 0.42 points, narrow because the differential between prestige and mass growth is a stable series (US prestige hair plus 11 percent in dollars in H1 2026 against mass plus 7 percent with units falling). What would move the read is the mass-market spill-over of the science-led tiers, since clinical evidence at cosmetic concentrations is still thin, and the Olaplex line inside HCB, which has declined since its 2022 peak: the observables are the Circana prestige-versus-mass split and L'Oréal's Professional Products growth (plus 12.6 percent like-for-like in H1 2026), and two half-years in which mass hair value grows faster than prestige would argue for the lower end of the range.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r04": "Weight in the answer: Clinical-efficacy premiumisation offsets 0.15 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the second largest tailwind of the 18 and 9 percent of the 1.7-point tailwind total. It is a hair-and-body driver with a light footprint in laundry where efficacy claims are regulated, so it reaches 31 percent of the pool; the Consumer force's pass-through and overlap correction treat it like any other of its drivers.\n\nWhere it lands: Hair care Europe (0.03 points, offsetting 7 percent of that cell's net contraction) and hair colour Europe (0.03 points) carry 37 percent of the effect; Europe holds 60 percent, because Directive (EU) 2024/825 raises the substantiation bar there first. It offsets 8 percent of the hair care contraction and 6 percent of body care. It sits on the same cells as C-03 (hair-care premiumisation), of which it is the proof-based sub-story, and it should not be added to C-03 as independent upside: the overlap correction already discounts the two together, and the claim-substantiation cost that accompanies it sits in G-05 and G-03.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2031, the pace of a claim standard spreading from prestige into mass: 53 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 96 percent by 2030, the half-way point is 2028 and the effect is complete by 2031. The one dated element is Directive (EU) 2024/825, applying from 27 September 2026, which raises the substantiation bar for every claim and so favours the tiers that already carry proof; the pace after that is the mass-market spill-over, the open part of the driver.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.10 to 0.20 points. The width is whether proof-based claims lift mass price points or only prestige: the prestige and dermo data confirm the direction (L'Oréal Dermatological Beauty plus 11.3 percent and Professional Products plus 12.6 percent like-for-like in H1 2026, US prestige hair serums up nearly 60 percent), while European body care, where clean without clinical declined in H1 2026, is the counter-signal. What to watch is NIQ's premium survey (56 percent of Western European shoppers require premium to prove features or performance), the mass-market price ladder in hair care once the directive applies, when the directive applies, and the Olaplex and Gliss Scalp Balance prints inside HCB.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r05": "Weight in the answer: Ageing consumers offset 0.13 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the fourth largest tailwind of the 18 and 7 percent of the 1.7-point tailwind total. The driver reaches 33 percent of the pool: its mechanism is grey coverage and gentle care, so hair colour carries it and the laundry and home-care categories carry only the small ageing-household slice. Pass-through and overlap treat it like any other Consumer driver, and the magnitude is deliberately a net one: the grey-acceptance offset is inside the score, not on top of it, which is why a certain demographic produces a modest expansion. The linear ramp to the horizon means the driver is only half present at 2030 and 98 percent present at 2035.\n\nWhere it lands: Hair colour Europe carries 29 percent of the effect (0.04 points, offsetting 7 percent of that cell's net contraction) and Europe holds 63 percent of the total; in hair colour North America the driver offsets 10 percent of the cell's contraction, a larger share of a smaller cell. It offsets 7 percent of the hair colour contraction and 4 percent of hair care.\n\nWhen it lands: A demographic drift, linear to 2035 with no shock and no plateau inside the horizon: 31 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 51 percent by 2030, the half-way point is 2030, and the driver is still growing in the last year of the horizon.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.09 to 0.16 points; the demographic timing is fixed and the width is the net colour effect, which could be half the modelled size if grey acceptance spreads or larger with grey-blending innovation. The series to watch are the colourant and styling lines in HCB's own hair prints (the strongest contributions to consumer hair growth in H1 2026), the US salon colour series (flat in 2024) and the over-55 share in dupe buying, which showed the largest increase between 2024 and 2026; two years of flat colourant growth against a growing over-65 population would argue for the lower end of the range.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r06": "Weight in the answer: The cost-of-living squeeze carries 0.69 of the 5.6 points, the third largest contribution of the 51 and 33 percent of the Consumer headwind. It is one of the broadest drivers in the base, present in every category and every region, and still reaches only 56 percent of the pool, because the categories that take trade-down first, laundry additives, fabric care, auto dishwash and fabric clean, are where it is most present, and the large hair colour cell is where it is least. Pass-through and overlap are the standard Consumer values.\n\nWhere it lands: Fabric clean Europe (0.11 points) and fabric clean North America (0.07 points) carry a quarter of the effect between them; 60 percent of it is European and 23 percent North American. Its category signature is the premium-format laundry lines: it accounts for 17 percent of the laundry additives contraction, 16 percent of fabric care and 15 percent of fabric finishers. In the European laundry cells it sits next to C-01 (private label) and in the North American ones next to C-34 (household formation) and G-15 (SNAP retrenchment); C-01 and this driver are one inflation complex and the overlap correction already discounts them together.\n\nWhen it lands: The most front-loaded of the five largest drivers: 83 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027 and 95 percent by 2028, the half-way point is 2026 and nothing is added after 2030. The second round is modelled as an energy-shock episode that fades if oil normalises in 2027 on the World Bank path, while the value-seeking habit it entrenches persists; that is why the ramp is steep and the peak early.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.52 to 0.81 points and it accounts for 10 percent of the portfolio band's variance. The width is the oil and rates path over 2026 and 2027, the same dependence E-12 (the feedstock shock) carries, so the two should be read as one oil-path exposure: a ceasefire or a longer war moves both. The observables are euro-area inflation (3.3 percent in August 2026, energy plus 14.3 percent), the ECB's path after the June 2026 hike and HCB's own price and volume prints, where Q2 2026 showed price minus 0.5 percent against volume plus 2.1 percent: two quarters of positive price with stable volume would argue for a smaller effect, a second energy leg for a larger one.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r07": "Weight in the answer: Scalp care as a regimen step offsets 0.05 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the 14th largest tailwind of the 18. It is a hair-care driver with a small expected effect that reaches 17 percent of the pool; pass-through and overlap are the ordinary Consumer values.\n\nWhere it lands: Hair care Europe carries 28 percent of the effect (0.01 points) and hair colour Europe 18 percent; Europe holds 52 percent of the total, North America 24 percent, where the measured shift is, and Asia 10 percent, where the regimen is already established in Japan and Korea so that the increment over the horizon is smaller. It offsets 5 percent of the hair care contraction and little elsewhere. It sits on the same cells as C-03 (hair-care premiumisation), of which it is one regimen-step sub-story next to C-04 (clinical efficacy) and C-10 (thinning); the four are one science-led hair story and should be read together, not added.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2031 that assumes the regimen step keeps spreading at the pace of the last three years of double-digit prestige growth: 53 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 96 percent by 2030, the half-way point is 2028 and the effect is complete by 2031. There is no dated gate; what would move the timing is the segment's share of salon retail, since a category that stays at today's share would arrive later and smaller than the curve assumes.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.03 to 0.07 points. The segment has three consecutive years of double-digit growth in US prestige (19 percent in H1 2025) and every major has a scalp flagship (Dove Scalp + Hair Therapy, Head & Shoulders, Vichy Dercos, and HCB's Gliss Scalp Balance since February 2026), but it is still 3.4 percent of US salon retail and could stay a niche rather than become a regimen step by 2031; the microbiome layer beneath it rests on a thin clinical base of three randomised trials. What to watch is the Circana prestige scalp series and the Kline share of salon retail; a scalp share of salon retail above 5 percent would argue for the upper end of the range, a third year of flat share for the lower end.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r10": "Weight in the answer: Medicalised hair-thinning demand offsets 0.05 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the 16th largest tailwind of the 18. It is a hair-care driver whose expected effect is among the smallest of the tailwinds, because the prescription and supplement tiers are captured by telehealth and Nutrafol and only cosmetic thickening is left to HCB; it reaches 19 percent of the pool, and nothing in the pass-through or the overlap correction sets it apart from the other Consumer drivers.\n\nWhere it lands: Hair care Europe and hair colour Europe carry 48 percent of the effect between them; Europe holds 55 percent of the total and North America 21 percent. It offsets 4 percent of the hair care contraction and 2 percent of hair colour, where grey-blending routines, root cover and colour-matched thickening fibres are colour products. It sits on the same cells as C-03 (hair-care premiumisation), C-07 (scalp care) and C-02 (GLP-1 demand), and the GLP-1 shedding occasion enters the numbers through C-02's magnitude while this driver carries the thinning regimen that responds to it; the four are one science-led hair story, to be read together rather than added.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2031 set by the pace of medical adoption rather than by anything dated: 53 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 96 percent by 2030, the half-way point is 2028 and the effect is complete by 2031. The demand is already measurable in the telehealth and supplement channels, so the early part of the curve is the cosmetic-thickening response lagging the medical one; a faster launch cadence in the cosmetic tier would pull the half-way point forward.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.03 to 0.06 points. The demand is documented (JAAD cohorts on GLP-1-associated hair loss, Unilever marketing Nutrafol as a complement to a GLP-1 regimen, Hims & Hers at USD 2.35bn of revenue with hair loss a founding specialty) and the width is the addressable cosmetic slice, which could be a fraction of the total demand growth. The observables are the share of thinning demand served by prescription and supplement channels against cosmetic thickening (Schwarzkopf, Gliss, BC Scalp Genesis, Men Perfect, Syoss Men) and the men's scalp and grey-blending lines; a cosmetic thickening segment that grows in line with the medical channel would argue for the upper end of the range.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r11": "Weight in the answer: The dupe economy carries 0.09 of the 5.6 points, the 31st largest contribution of the 51 and 4 percent of the Consumer headwind. It is a hair-and-body driver most present in hair care and styling, the most duped segments, and reaches 23 percent of the pool; the laundry and home-care dupe is own label and sits in C-01, so nothing of this driver lands there. The Consumer pass-through and overlap correction treat it like any other driver of the force.\n\nWhere it lands: Hair care Europe carries 21 percent of the effect (0.02 points) and hair colour Europe 13 percent; Europe holds 51 percent of the total and North America 27 percent, where the norm is measured. It accounts for 6 percent of the hair care contraction, 5 percent of hair styling and 4 percent of body care. It sits on the same cells as C-03 (hair-care premiumisation) and C-35 (mass unit decline), and it works against C-03 in particular: a willingness-to-pay ceiling that collapses to the cheapest credible equivalent is the constraint on the premium tier's price ladder, so the two should be read as one price-premium question.\n\nWhen it lands: Front-loaded to 2029, because the behaviour is already mainstream and the remaining shift is the concave last third of the adoption curve: 73 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027, 87 percent by 2028, the half-way point is 2026 and the effect is complete by 2030.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.06 to 0.12 points; the norm is measured (58 percent of US shoppers have bought a dupe, 70 percent of beauty buyers would even if they could afford the original, the fastest 2024 to 2026 increase among consumers over 55), and the width is the premium erosion it causes, which depends on how prestige responds. HCB is exposed from both ends, since Olaplex is one of the most duped prestige hair franchises while Gliss and Syoss gain as the credible equivalent, and the net of that is inside the score. The evidence to follow is the Circana dupe survey series and the price gap between prestige and mass bond-repair lines; a narrowing gap driven by prestige price cuts would argue for the upper end of the range.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r16": "Weight in the answer: China domestic-brand capture carries 0.02 of the 5.6 points, the smallest headwind of the 33. Its expected effect sits in the lower third of the base and its reach is the smallest of any driver, 5 percent of the pool, because it is a hair driver whose presence is Asian, a region that holds a twentieth of the estimated mix, with only a Middle East spill-over outside it, and because the model counts only the increment over the horizon of a share shift that is already two thirds realised. Pass-through and overlap are the ordinary Consumer values.\n\nWhere it lands: 62 percent of the effect is in Asia and the rest in High Growth through a Middle East spill-over; hair care Asia carries 34 percent of it and hair colour Asia 18 percent, and in those two cells it accounts for 12 and 13 percent of the cell shift, larger shares of very small cells. It accounts for 1 percent of the hair care contraction. It sits on the Asian hair cells with C-03 (hair-care premiumisation) and C-37 (growth-market expansion), whose Asian part it works against.\n\nWhen it lands: Front-loaded to 2029: 72 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027, 88 percent by 2028 and the effect is complete by 2030; the remaining shift is a tail.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range is one of the narrowest in the base, 0.01 to 0.02 points, because the residual effect is bounded by the share already lost (domestic brands at 57.4 percent of China's cosmetics market in 2025, the fifth consecutive year of gains). What would change the read is a stall of the tail, and the observables are the domestic-brand share series (CAFFCI) and the foreign brands' China prints: P&G regained share in China after 15 quarters and Henkel's Asia-Pacific region grew very strongly in H1 2026 driven by hair, so two more half-years of foreign-brand recovery would argue for re-scoring the tail to nil. The reader should hold against the rank that HCB's China hair exposure is larger than the driver's weight suggests (Vidal Sassoon Greater China, Schwarzkopf, Syoss, a plant expansion in the 2026 capex list): the driver is small because the pool slice is small, not because the market is.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r18": "Weight in the answer: The delayed US Hispanic demographic dividend offsets 0.06 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the eleventh largest tailwind of the 18 and 4 percent of the 1.7-point tailwind total. It is a North America-only driver and reaches 16 percent of the pool; the Consumer pass-through and overlap correction treat it like any other driver of the force, and its weight is set by the one region it touches and by a modest magnitude. Timing holds down the path rather than the terminal value, since the dividend is modelled to start only after the 2025 to 2027 enforcement and migration shock.\n\nWhere it lands: All of the effect is North American; fabric clean North America carries 45 percent of it (0.03 points), and in the small North American hair cells it offsets 10 percent of the colour cell's net contraction and 10 percent of the care cell's, larger shares of smaller cells, because Hispanic households over-index on hair care and laundry (NIQ indices 138 and 126). It sits on the same cells as C-34 (the immigration reversal), and the two are one demographic story: the same migration drop that delays this dividend is the near-term contraction there, and the net of the pair is what North American volume shows.\n\nWhen it lands: The onset is 2027 and the ramp is linear to 2033, a drift rather than an adoption: nothing in 2026, 18 percent of the terminal effect by 2028, 55 percent by 2030, the half-way point in 2030, and 99 percent expected materialisation at 2035 because the peak can slip past the horizon.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.03 to 0.09 points, from about half of the expected value to two fifths above it, one of the wider ranges relative to size in the base, because the policy path decides both the size of the dividend and its onset. The demographics are certain (70.1 million Hispanic Americans delivering 76 percent of US population growth in 2025); the enforcement and migration regime is not, and 2025 already showed Hispanic per-household spending growth slowing to 0.8 percent with unacculturated households at minus 4.8 percent. What to watch is the Census migration series, Numerator's Hispanic spending series and the remittance-tax effect from January 2026; a policy turn on migration is what would bring the onset forward, and the absence of one by 2027 is what would push it back.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r24": "Weight in the answer: Textured and curly hair as a mainstream category offsets 0.05 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the 15th largest tailwind of the 18. It is a hair-care and styling driver whose regional profile is North America and High Growth, so it reaches 16 percent of the pool; nothing in the pass-through or the overlap correction sets it apart from the other Consumer drivers.\n\nWhere it lands: The effect is split three ways, 34 percent High Growth, 33 percent Europe and 30 percent North America, an unusual shape for a hair tailwind, because the growth-market mainstreaming (Africa, the Middle East, Brazil) is where the driver is most present, while in Europe it is limited to the UK and France. Hair care High Growth carries 18 percent of it, and in hair care North America it offsets 8 percent of the cell's net contraction. It offsets 4 percent of the hair care contraction and 3 percent of hair styling. It sits on the same cells as C-03 (hair-care premiumisation) and C-37 (growth-market expansion) and should be read as a category-creation sub-story of both rather than added to them.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2032: 35 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 90 percent by 2030, the half-way point is 2029 and the effect is complete by 2032.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.03 to 0.06 points. The category is mainstream in the US (curl care the only US salon-retail segment that did not decline in 2024; textured-hair consumers spend about 78 percent more on hair products) and the width is the growth-market expansion, which could add materially or not at all for HCB's range, since Not Your Mother's Curl Talk is a North American mass curl line and HCB has no type-4 or coily range. The series to watch are the Kline salon-retail series and the Mintel textured-hair series; the capture caveat is that without a coily or Africa-relevant range the High Growth part of the effect is category growth that competitors take.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r25": "Weight in the answer: Sub-replacement fertility and population decline carry 0.13 of the 5.6 points, the 23rd largest contribution of the 51 and 6 percent of the Consumer headwind. The driver reaches 45 percent of the pool, a broad reach through the volume categories, and its small weight comes from a deliberately small magnitude: it carries volume only, the fewer-children channel and the post-2029 population decline, because the household-count story that would make it larger is not there, as EU household counts still rise to 2035. Inside the horizon the curve is back-loaded, so the driver is a fraction of its terminal value in every year before 2032 and even at 2035 only 96 percent of it is expected to be present.\n\nWhere it lands: Fabric clean Europe carries 24 percent of the effect (0.03 points), because laundry is the volume category and Europe is where the fertility decline is measured; Europe holds 72 percent of the total and Asia 8 percent, a comparatively high Asian share for the base, since Japan, Korea and China are already shrinking. Its category share is small everywhere, 3 percent of the hand dishwash contraction at most. It sits on the European laundry cells with C-01 (private label) and C-06 (the cost-of-living squeeze), and it should be read as a volume floor beneath those price drivers rather than added to them.\n\nWhen it lands: The most gradual driver in the base, with the lowest share of its terminal effect in place at 2030 of any driver: 10 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 27 percent by 2030, 52 percent by 2032, and the half-way point is only 2032. The curve is deliberately convex: the fewer-children channel is already measurable, but the EU population peaks at 453 million in 2029 and the decline that follows is what dominates the late years.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.09 to 0.16 points; the demographic timing is fixed and the width is the net volume effect inside the horizon, which atomisation and still-rising household counts could partly offset, since EU household size has been flat at 2.3 since 2014 and German household counts still rise to 2035. The observables are the Eurostat fertility and EUROPOP series (EU fertility 1.34 in 2024, the lowest in the series), the Destatis household projection, and the Eurostat live-birth counts. A revision of the 2029 population peak in either direction is the trigger to re-score; nothing that happens in a single year should.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r27": "Weight in the answer: Dishwasher penetration catch-up offsets 0.01 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the smallest tailwind of the 18 and the smallest contribution in the base. Its reach is 6 percent of the pool, one category with a small share of the mix plus a trace in hand dishwash, and its expected effect is small because the catch-up that matters for Somat and Pril is Southern and Eastern Europe, Türkiye and MEA, while the large low-penetration markets (China at about 3 percent of households, India, Brazil) have no HCB auto dishwash franchise and Finish absorbs most of the migration there.\n\nWhere it lands: Auto dishwash Europe carries 84 percent of the effect on its own, and 91 percent of the total is European. It offsets 2 percent of the auto dishwash contraction; the hand dishwash cells carry a small positive echo rather than the volume the appliance takes from them, because the driver is modelled as expansion and a cannibalised category cannot carry a negative score, so that loss is kept small by construction rather than modelled. It sits on the European auto dishwash cell with C-01 (private label), X-15 (scale-leader escalation) and C-06 (the squeeze), the three largest items in a cell that is one of the most exposed in the mix, and it offsets a few percent of them.\n\nWhen it lands: A linear ramp to 2034, because appliance replacement cycles are slow and steady: 34 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 57 percent by 2030, the half-way point is 2030, and the driver is still growing in the last year of the horizon with 99 percent expected materialisation at 2035.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.01 to 0.02 points; penetration growth of one to two points a year could be twice or half that and there is no dated trigger. What to watch is the Destatis and national appliance-ownership surveys (Germany at 77.3 percent of households) and Henkel's dishwashing prints (very strong growth in Q3 2025, double-digit hand dishwash growth in H1 2026); the read would change materially only if HCB built an auto dishwash franchise in a low-penetration market, which is a management decision outside the model.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r28": "Weight in the answer: Scent-led premiumisation of additives and finishers offsets 0.07 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the eighth largest tailwind of the 18 and 4 percent of the 1.7-point tailwind total. It reaches 19 percent of the pool, because it sits almost entirely in the laundry additives and finishers that carry it and in the fragranced variants of detergent; pass-through and overlap are the ordinary Consumer values.\n\nWhere it lands: In points the laundry-detergent cells lead (fabric clean Europe 0.02, fabric clean North America 0.01) because they are the largest cells, but the driver's identity is in the small categories: it offsets 6 percent of the laundry additives contraction and 5 percent of fabric finishers, its largest shares of any category. Europe holds 55 percent of the effect and North America 32 percent, where the Unstopables franchise leads the segment. It sits on the same cells as C-29 (garment care occasions), which is the adjacent fabric-care occasion, and the regulatory cost of its mechanism sits in G-02: the encapsulate restriction of 17 October 2029 lands on the same scent-premium pool, so the two should be read as one scent story with a cost inside it.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2030: 72 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 93 percent by 2029, the half-way point is 2028 and the effect is complete by 2031.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.05 to 0.10 points. The segment is structural and Henkel's own Fabric Care prints confirm it (double-digit growth in Q3 2025, significant growth in Q1 and Q2 2026 while Fabric Cleaning declined), but two things could halve HCB's capture: P&G's lead through Downy and Lenor Unstopables, and the 2029 encapsulate gate, which Silan already meets with microplastic-free perfumes. The series to watch are the booster market series (EUR 1.9bn in 2023 heading to EUR 3bn by 2028), Henkel's Fabric Care growth by quarter and the price points of laundry perfumes (Silan's first at EUR 4.99 in Austria); a quarter in which Fabric Care growth falls back to the category rate would argue for the lower end of the range.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r29": "Weight in the answer: Garment care beyond cleaning offsets 0.07 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the tenth largest tailwind of the 18 and 4 percent of the 1.7-point tailwind total. It reaches 17 percent of the pool: specialty fabric care and finishers carry it in full, additives partly, and nothing outside laundry. The Consumer force's pass-through and overlap correction treat it like any other of its drivers.\n\nWhere it lands: In points the laundry-detergent cells lead because of their size, but the driver's identity is in fabric care, where it offsets 6 percent of the contraction, and fabric finishers (5 percent). Europe holds 62 percent of the effect and North America 28 percent, the largest fabric-enhancer, refresh and cold-cycle market. It sits on the same cells as C-28 (scent-led premiumisation), the adjacent fabric-care occasion; the two are one specialty-care story with two mechanisms, fibre care here and scent there, and should not be added as independent upside.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2031 whose early years are already visible in Henkel's Fabric Care prints: 53 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 96 percent by 2030, the half-way point is 2028 and the effect is complete by 2031. Nothing in the timing is a gate, since the one dated element, the EU ban on destroying unsold textiles from 19 July 2026, acts on awareness rather than on sales; the refresh sub-occasion is what would make the late years larger.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.04 to 0.09 points, from about two thirds of the expected value to a third above it, because the occasion shift is measured in surveys rather than in sales. Henkel's own prints confirm the occasion (Fabric Care, Perwoll's category, grew double-digit in Q3 2025 and significantly in Q1 and Q2 2026 while Fabric Cleaning declined), the between-wash refresh occasion is real but unquantified, and EU textile policy acts only as a slow awareness driver for longevity norms. What to watch is Henkel's Fabric Care growth by quarter, the polyester share of fibre output (59 percent in 2024) and the ownership of Woolite under Vestacy; two quarters of Fabric Care growth at the category rate would argue for the lower end of the range.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r34": "Weight in the answer: The US immigration reversal carries 0.07 of the 5.6 points, the 38th largest contribution of the 51 and 3 percent of the Consumer headwind. It is a North America-only volume driver, so it reaches 17 percent of the pool; the Consumer pass-through and overlap correction treat it like any other driver of the force, and its weight is set by the one region it touches.\n\nWhere it lands: All of the effect is North American and fabric clean North America carries 54 percent of it (0.04 points, 6 percent of that cell's shift); body care North America carries 17 percent, since household counts drive volume in laundry (all, Persil, Snuggle), dish and Dial body care. It accounts for 3 percent of the body care contraction and 2 percent of fabric clean. It sits on the North American cells with C-01 (private label), C-06 (the squeeze) and C-18 (the Hispanic dividend), and its relationship with C-18 is the one to hold in mind: the same migration drop that cuts household formation here delays the dividend there, so the two are one demographic story with a near-term contraction and a delayed expansion.\n\nWhen it lands: Linear to 2029, because the household shortfall accumulates every year the low migration flow persists: 55 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027, 77 percent by 2028, the half-way point is 2027 and the effect is complete by 2031.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.04 to 0.09 points; the migration drop is measured (net international migration from 2.7 million in 2024 to 1.3 million in 2025, 0.3 million projected for 2026) and the width is the size of the HCB volume effect, which is inferred from household-formation projections, and the enforcement intensity of 2026 and 2027. The series to watch are the Census migration estimates, the Joint Center's household-growth series (its low series at 690,000 a year) and young-adult headship (32.7 percent in 2024); a policy turn that lifts migration back above 1 million a year would argue for the lower end of the range and would bring C-18's onset forward.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r35": "Weight in the answer: Mass hair and body unit decline carries 0.12 of the 5.6 points, the 25th largest contribution of the 51 and 6 percent of the Consumer headwind. It is a hair-and-body driver most present in North America and reaches 24 percent of the pool; nothing in the pass-through or the overlap correction sets it apart from the other Consumer drivers. It is the contraction counterpart of C-03 (hair-care premiumisation) and exists because the driver grammar is one-sided: C-03 carries the premium upside, this line carries the mass-shelf loss with the correct sign, and the net of the two is the value migration the reader is looking for.\n\nWhere it lands: Hair care Europe (0.02 points) and hair colour Europe (0.02 points) carry 38 percent of the effect; North America holds 31 percent of the total, a high share for a hair driver, because that is where the mass unit decline is measured (US mass hair plus 7 percent in dollars in H1 2026 with units down, against prestige plus 11 percent with units plus 8 percent). It accounts for 7 percent of the hair care contraction, 6 percent of body care and 5 percent of hair styling. It sits on the same cells as C-03, C-01 (private label) and C-06 (the squeeze); the overlap correction discounts them together.\n\nWhen it lands: A linear ramp to 2031: 52 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 84 percent by 2030, the half-way point is 2028 and the effect is complete by 2033.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.07 to 0.16 points and it accounts for 2 percent of the portfolio band's variance; the pace of mass unit decline could be half or double depending on prestige and own-label substitution. The series to watch are the Circana mass-versus-prestige unit series, the Kline channel shares (e-commerce gained 15.6 points of hair-care sales over 2019 to 2024 while salon back bar and take-home both lost share) and HCB's European body care, where Fa declined in H1 2026: a half-year in which mass units stabilise while prestige keeps growing would argue for the lower end of the range, since it would mean the value is migrating without the volume.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r36": "Weight in the answer: The euro translation haircut on growth-market gross profit carries 0.23 of the 5.6 points, the eleventh largest contribution of the 51. It is the one driver fully present in every category, and it still reaches only 31 percent of the pool, because a translation loss lands where the currencies are: the High Growth region holds a fifth of the estimated mix and Europe and Asia carry only the currency fringe, so the driver's reach is set by geography, not by its category profile. Pass-through and overlap are the ordinary Consumer values.\n\nWhere it lands: 64 percent of the effect sits in High Growth and 33 percent in Europe (the CEE currencies); North America carries none of it. Its largest cells are hair colour High Growth (0.03 points, 32 percent of that cell's shift), hair care High Growth (0.03 points, 29 percent) and fabric clean High Growth (0.02 points, 18 percent): in ten of the thirteen High Growth cells this driver is the largest single item outright, in laundry additives it ties C-06 (the squeeze), and in hair care and fabric clean it trails X-15 (by a hundredth of a point in hair care, four hundredths in fabric clean); it is the largest reason why most High Growth cells contract more than their Asian peers, where the currency haircut barely registers. Its most visible category share is insect control, 14 percent of the contraction, the category with the highest High Growth weight in the mix. It sits on the same cells as C-37 (growth-market expansion), the organic growth it deflates, and the two are read together: C-37 states the growth in organic terms and this driver is the euro correction to it.\n\nWhen it lands: Front-loaded to 2030, because depreciation and inflation are decelerating (the lira lost 20.6 percent on average in 2025 and a further 9.8 percent by September 2026), so the haircut is heaviest now: 65 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027, 79 percent by 2028, the half-way point is 2027 and the effect is complete by 2031.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.17 to 0.27 points. The haircut is observed now (IMEA grew 14.9 percent organically in H1 2026 while nominal sales were flat, and the H1 2026 FX effect on Consumer Brands was minus 4.1 points), so the question is persistence, not existence; currency paths in Türkiye, Egypt and Latin America are among the least predictable inputs in the base. The observables are the ECB reference rate for the lira, Turkish and Egyptian inflation (31.8 and 14.9 percent in July 2026), the IAS 29 monetary loss in Henkel's notes (EUR 39m in 2025) and the regional EBIT table: a year of lira stability would argue for the lower end of the range. A change of convention would remove the driver altogether: it exists because the pool is read in euro terms, and a constant-currency reading would have no place for it.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "consumer_r37": "Weight in the answer: Growth-market expansion offsets 0.13 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the third largest tailwind of the 18 and 8 percent of the 1.7-point tailwind total. It is the clearest case in the base of the mix deciding the weight: the driver is present across almost every category, and it reaches only 18 percent of the pool, because it is confined by construction to the High Growth region and the growth parts of Asia, and those hold a quarter of the estimated mix between them. The Consumer pass-through and overlap correction treat it like any other driver of the force.\n\nWhere it lands: 85 percent of the effect is in High Growth and the rest in Asia; Europe and North America carry none of it. Hair care High Growth (0.03 points, offsetting 29 percent of that cell's net contraction), hair colour High Growth (0.02 points, 26 percent) and fabric clean High Growth (0.02 points, 14 percent) carry 54 percent of it, and in those cells it is the largest offset to C-36 (the euro translation haircut), which sits on the same cells and deflates the same growth. Read the two together: this driver states the growth in organic terms, C-36 is the euro correction, and the net of the pair is what the High Growth cells actually show. Its largest category share is insect control, 8 percent of the contraction offset, the category with the highest High Growth weight in the mix.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2032: 35 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 90 percent by 2030, the half-way point is 2029 and the effect is complete by 2032.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.08 to 0.18 points. The growth is in the prints (IMEA plus 12.1 percent organic in 2025, plus 17.4 percent in Q2 2026), and the width is the peak level, which the Gulf war effect on consumption and Türkiye's macro path make uncertain; growth could halve in a prolonged conflict. What to watch is Henkel's IMEA and Asia-Pacific organic growth by quarter, Gulf consumption after the war (Saudi Arabia resilient so far), Indonesia's halal mandate for cosmetics from 17 October 2026, and the Indian footprint after the Pril and Fa licences ended on 31 May 2026: two quarters of IMEA growth below 10 percent would argue for the lower end of the range.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "customer_r01": "Weight in the answer: Discount channel share gain carries 0.41 of the 5.6 points, the sixth largest contribution of the 51 and 27 percent of the Customer headwind. It reaches 57 percent of the pool, most present in the laundry categories and additives and least in hair, and the overlap correction takes 17 percent because it shares its European laundry and dish cells with K-13 (retail media), K-03 (buyer power) and K-12 (drugstores), the same channel-terms complex.\n\nWhere it lands: Fabric clean Europe (0.08 points) and fabric clean North America (0.04 points) carry 29 percent of the effect; 65 percent of it is European, 19 percent North American and 15 percent in High Growth, where BIM, A101, Sok and the Mexican and Egyptian discounters give the driver a larger High Growth share than any other Customer headwind. It is an even share of the laundry and home-care categories, 9 percent of the insect control contraction and 9 percent of hard-surface cleaner, and a small share of hair, where the channel matters less. The mechanism inside the pool is trade-margin dilution and price-ladder compression, not delisting: the discounter reference price caps the category ladder, which is why the additives and laundry categories carry the most.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2029 that is steep in the middle: 53 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027, 92 percent by 2028 and the effect is complete by 2029. That timing reflects a German market where the gain plateaued in 2025 against a UK, US and Southern European market still gaining.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.33 to 0.47 points and it accounts for 5 percent of the portfolio band's variance; the pace is bounded by published store-opening plans, which is why the range is narrow. The observables are the German channel share (discounters at 46.6 percent of food-retail revenue), Lidl GB's UK share (a record 8.4 percent in April 2026), Aldi US's store count against its 3,200 target for 2028, and the non-food share taken by the variety discounters: a second year of German plateau with US expansion on plan leaves the read where it is, a renewed German gain would argue for the upper half of the range.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "customer_r02": "Weight in the answer: E-commerce and marketplace share gain carries 0.18 of the 5.6 points, the 14th largest contribution of the 51 and 12 percent of the Customer headwind. It reaches 46 percent of the pool, most present in auto dishwash and laundry, the archetypal subscribe-and-save items, and it is held down by two things: the Customer pass-through (7.0 percent) and an overlap correction of 16 percent, since it shares its laundry cells with K-13 (retail media), K-01 (discounters) and K-03 (buyer power).\n\nWhere it lands: Fabric clean North America is its largest cell (0.04 points, 20 percent of the effect), and North America holds 38 percent of the effect against 44 percent for Europe: the channel is still growing at double digits in the US (Walmart US e-commerce plus 24 percent, Amazon net sales plus 20 percent in Q2 2026) while continental Europe is maturing at a low single-digit online share of FMCG. Asia holds 8 percent despite the same high exposure as North America, because the region is a twentieth of the pool. Its category share is small everywhere, 4 percent of the insect control contraction at most, the profile of a margin driver rather than a demand driver.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2031 that follows the American channel rather than the European one: 53 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 96 percent by 2030, the half-way point is 2028 and the effect is complete by 2031. The American channel is still growing at double-digit rates while the continental European one is maturing, so the European part of the effect arrives later than the American one and the curve is an average of the two.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.11 to 0.24 points and it accounts for 4 percent of the portfolio band's variance; the width is the net-margin gap between marketplace and store sales, which is inferred from fees and media rather than disclosed and could be half or double the modelled value. What would move the read is the replenishment layer: Alexa for Shopping's Scheduled Actions (May 2026) and the club channel, where default status fixes share at the moment of subscription, because a driver that fixes share also fixes the margin gap. The series to watch are Amazon's and Walmart's e-commerce growth, Schwarz Group's online revenue (flat at EUR 1.7bn in FY2025) and NIQ's ranking of Amazon in European online beauty, number one in eight of ten countries.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "customer_r03": "Weight in the answer: European retailer buyer power carries 0.27 of the 5.6 points, the ninth largest contribution of the 51 and 18 percent of the Customer headwind. It reaches 47 percent of the pool: the exposure is broad across laundry, dish and colour, but the driver is European by construction, since the alliances and cross-border sourcing have no documented spill-over into other regions, so nearly half the pool sits outside it. The overlap correction takes 17 percent, the Customer rate, because it shares its cells with K-01 (discounters), K-13 (retail media) and K-12 (drugstores).\n\nWhere it lands: 84 percent of the effect is European. Fabric clean Europe carries 23 percent of it (0.06 points), hair colour Europe 16 percent (0.04 points): laundry because it is the largest negotiated category, colour because cross-border sourcing targets identical pan-European SKUs such as Palette, Syoss and Taft, whereas laundry is split into national brands. Its category share is modest everywhere, 7 percent of the hair colour contraction at most, which is what a margin-terms driver spread across many categories looks like.\n\nWhen it lands: A linear ramp to 2030: 64 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 93 percent by 2030, the half-way point is 2028. The alliances re-forming in 2025 and 2026 (Concordis, Eurelec) and the store conversions of October 2026 set the early years; the regulation that would accelerate it is not yet tabled.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.17 to 0.36 points and it accounts for 6 percent of the portfolio band's variance, the ordinary width for a driver of its size, and one decision sits inside it: a regulation on territorial supply constraints, which the EU Single Market Strategy of May 2025 committed to tackle, would double the effect by letting alliances buy across borders, and its absence halves it. That proposal is the observable; the secondary ones are the German concentration series (Edeka and Rewe at 46.8 percent of grocery) and the Ahold Delhaize and Eurelec statements on unexplained price differences between countries.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "customer_r04": "Weight in the answer: Social commerce as a scaled beauty channel offsets 0.06 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the twelfth largest tailwind of the 18 and the second tailwind in the Customer force. It is a hair-only driver and reaches 28 percent of the pool; the Customer pass-through (7.0 percent) and an overlap correction of 18 percent, shared with K-03 (buyer power), K-13 (retail media) and K-12 (drugstores) on the European hair cells, hold a driver with a modest expected effect in the lower half of the tailwinds.\n\nWhere it lands: Hair colour Europe (0.01 points) and hair care Europe (0.01 points) carry 35 percent of the effect; Europe holds 49 percent of the total, North America 23 percent and Asia and High Growth together 28 percent, a comparatively high share outside the two large regions, where the channel's live-commerce formats are most advanced. It offsets 4 percent of the hair styling contraction, 3 percent of hair colour and 3 percent of hair care. Its counterpart is X-16 (challenger fragmentation) in the Competitive force: this driver carries the new occasions and impulse volume the channel creates, X-16 carries the share the channel's native brands take, and the net of the pair is what the channel does to HCB.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2030: 72 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 93 percent by 2029, the half-way point is 2028 and the effect is complete by 2031.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.04 to 0.08 points. The channel is scaled (TikTok Shop US GMV USD 15.1bn in 2025 and USD 11.8bn in H1 2026, beauty and personal care its largest category, ten European markets live since June 2026) and the width is HCB's share of it, which is the open point, together with the margin caveat of platform commission plus 10 to 20 percent affiliate fees. The observables are Circana's TikTok Shop beauty series, the European GMV estimates (about EUR 500m across four continental markets in the 90 days to 9 July 2026) and the share of GMV from live commerce, a minority format in the West at about 8 percent of US attributed GMV; a plateau in the beauty share would argue for the lower end of the range.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "customer_r07": "Weight in the answer: Professional and bond-repair brands crossing into retail offset 0.12 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the fifth largest tailwind of the 18 and the largest tailwind in the Customer force, where it is 65 percent of a small total. It is a hair driver with a high expected effect that reaches only 25 percent of the pool, and the Customer force takes back a further 18 percent through the overlap correction it shares with K-03 (buyer power), K-13 (retail media) and K-12 (drugstores) on the same European hair cells.\n\nWhere it lands: Hair colour Europe (0.03 points) and hair care Europe (0.02 points) carry 42 percent of the effect; in hair colour Asia it offsets 14 percent of the cell's net contraction, its largest share of any cell. Europe holds 54 percent of the total and North America 23 percent. It offsets 7 percent of the hair care contraction, 6 percent of hair colour and 6 percent of hair styling. It is the expansion side of a channel whose contraction side is X-18 (professional channel restructuring): the crossover lifts the retail price ladder while the salon channel it comes from erodes, and the net of the pair is what the professional business shows.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2030: 75 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 96 percent by 2029, the half-way point is 2028 and the effect is complete by 2030.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range is, relative to its size, as narrow as any tailwind's in the base, 0.10 to 0.14 points, because the crossover is documented brand by brand and its pace is bounded (L'Oréal Professional Products above EUR 5bn with 39 percent of sales outside salons, plus 12.6 percent like-for-like in H1 2026; Henkel's professional growth very strong in H1 2026). What the reader should hold against it is the capture caveat inside the driver: the crossover is led by L'Oréal PPD, Olaplex, K18 and Wella, so it raises category value that HCB rides through Olaplex and its own professional business rather than owns. The observables are PPD's outside-salon share and Olaplex's own retail trajectory inside HCB.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "customer_r12": "Weight in the answer: Drugstore channel share gain carries 0.15 of the 5.6 points, the 19th largest contribution of the 51 and 10 percent of the Customer headwind. It is a European-only driver most present in hair and body, the drugstores' categories, so it reaches 39 percent of the pool; the Customer pass-through (7.0 percent) and an overlap correction of 18 percent, shared with K-03 (buyer power), K-13 (retail media) and K-01 (discounters) on the same European cells, hold it in the middle of the ranking.\n\nWhere it lands: All of the effect is European. Hair colour Europe (0.04 points, 24 percent of the effect), fabric clean Europe (0.02 points) and hair care Europe (0.02 points) carry 55 percent of it. Its category share is highest in hair colour (5 percent of the contraction) and hair styling (4 percent), the categories where Balea, Isana, Denkmit and Domol sit directly against Schwarzkopf, Syoss, Fa, Persil and Somat at 30 to 50 percent price gaps.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2030: 75 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 96 percent by 2029, the half-way point is 2028 and the effect is complete by 2030.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range is narrow, 0.10 to 0.19 points, because the channel gain is a measured, steady series (drugstores posted the strongest 2025 sales growth of any stationary German food retailer at 6.6 percent). The open question is whether the pace holds and how much of the loyalty and app platforms (Payback at dm) turns into media inventory, which would move part of this driver's cost into the retail-media line, K-13, rather than add to it. The series to watch are NIQ's channel series, the drugstore share of packages switched away from grocers (nearly 70 percent in 2025) and the German own-label total, almost EUR 80bn in 2025.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "customer_r13": "Weight in the answer: Retail media carries 0.48 of the 5.6 points, the fourth largest contribution of the 51 and 33 percent of the Customer force's headwind, the largest item in that force. It is a global driver with a high expected effect that reaches 57 percent of the pool, laundry and additives above all, and two things hold it below the three above it: the Customer pass-through is lower (7.0 percent against 8.1 for the Consumer drivers) and the overlap correction is heavier than theirs, 17 percent, because it sits on the same laundry and hair cells as K-01 (discounters), K-03 (buyer power) and K-12 (drugstores).\n\nWhere it lands: The two laundry-detergent cells carry 32 percent of the effect: fabric clean North America (0.08 points, 14 percent of that cell's shift) and fabric clean Europe (0.07 points). North America holds 33 percent of the effect, a higher North American share than any of the other large drivers, because that is where the visibility tax is heaviest and where Amazon and Walmart take 89 percent of the increment. It is a mid-sized share of every category rather than a large share of any: 11 percent of the laundry additives contraction, 11 percent of hair care, 10 percent of insect control.\n\nWhen it lands: Front-loaded to 2029 as the growth of the channel decelerates from 18 percent towards low double digits: 72 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027, 88 percent by 2028 and the half-way point is already 2026. Nothing is added after 2030.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range is narrow, 0.39 to 0.56 points, and it accounts for 6 percent of the portfolio band's variance, because the take rates are disclosed by the retailers and the spend series is measured. What would move the read is the share of the spend that is incremental rather than a transfer: NIQ's finding that a campaign reporting 14 to 1 ROAS measured 0.4 to 1 under marketing-mix modelling is the reason the cost is modelled as a margin transfer, so a change in the measured incrementality, or a retailer pricing loyalty data separately from media, is the observable. The European figures (IAB Europe projects EUR 28.8bn by 2028) decide whether the European cells catch up with the North American ones.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "environmental_r01": "Weight in the answer: The lauric-oil and palm-derivative squeeze carries 0.24 of the 5.6 points, the tenth largest contribution of the 51 and 30 percent of the Environmental headwind. It is present in every region at nearly the same level, because the price is global, and reaches 52 percent of the pool; its ceiling is the Environmental pass-through, the lowest of the six forces at 6.3 percent, and the 24 percent overlap correction it shares with E-12 (the petro-feedstock shock), E-07 (European energy) and E-03 (carbon costs), the same cost stack.\n\nWhere it lands: The effect follows surfactant intensity rather than category size: hand dishwash, where it accounts for 8 percent of the contraction, is its largest category share, followed by hair care (7 percent) and body care (7 percent), the categories built on the C12 to C14 alcohols, SLES, betaines and glucosides that lauric oils feed. In points the largest cells are still the laundry ones, fabric clean Europe (0.03 points) and fabric clean North America (0.02 points), because they are the largest cells in the mix. Europe holds 53 percent of the effect, one of the lower European shares among the cost drivers, because the underlying price is the same everywhere and the exposure is nearly even across the regions; the EUDR compliance premium of USD 350 to 400 per tonne on crude palm kernel oil, which only EU buyers pay, is what keeps the European share slightly above the region's weight in the pool.\n\nWhen it lands: Front-loaded to 2027: 93 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027 and the effect is complete by 2029. The prices are already at these levels (palm kernel oil averaged USD 2,093 per tonne in 2025 against USD 990 in 2023) and Indonesia's B50 mandate has been live since 1 July 2026, so the remaining increment is the 2027 feedstock pull of a mandate whose 2026 allocation was left almost flat.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.18 to 0.29 points. The width is a commodity path, not a mechanism: Indonesia's B50 execution and its 2027 biodiesel allocation decide whether the pull that APROBI estimates at 17 to 18 million tonnes of crude palm oil a year arrives in full. The observables are the World Bank Pink Sheet series for palm kernel and coconut oil (palm kernel oil at USD 2,166 per tonne in July 2026, coconut oil averaging USD 2,480 in 2025), the allocation decision, and Henkel's direct-material guidance; a 2027 allocation held flat again would argue for the lower end of the range.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "environmental_r03": "Weight in the answer: EU carbon-cost pass-through carries 0.10 of the 5.6 points, the 29th largest contribution of the 51 and 13 percent of the Environmental headwind. It reaches 34 percent of the pool, a European driver most present in styling, colour, body and insect control, the aerosol and tube formats, and the Environmental force gives it the lowest pass-through of the six (6.3 percent) and an overlap correction of 27 percent shared with E-12 (the feedstock shock), E-01 (lauric oils) and E-07 (European energy).\n\nWhere it lands: 90 percent of the effect is European. Hair colour Europe (0.02 points) and fabric clean Europe (0.02 points) carry 41 percent of it; its highest category shares are hair styling (4 percent of the contraction) and insect control (4 percent), where aluminium aerosol cans and collapsible tubes fall under the proposed CBAM extension to downstream goods. The energy channel is deliberately not here; it sits in E-07, and the two should not be added as if the carbon price and the gas price were separate exposures.\n\nWhen it lands: A linear ramp to 2030 from dated steps: CBAM definitive from 1 January 2026, the proposed downstream extension from 1 January 2028, ETS2 fully operational in 2028. 61 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 96 percent by 2030, the half-way point is 2028 and the effect is complete by 2031.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range is narrow, 0.07 to 0.13 points, because CBAM and ETS2 are dated; the width is the EUA price path (around EUR 80 per tonne in July 2026, CBAM certificates at EUR 75 for Q2 2026) and one decision: the extension of CBAM to steel- and aluminium-intensive downstream products (COM(2025) 989, Council position agreed 12 June 2026), which decides whether cans and tubes carry the cost from 2028. That proposal's final scope and the free-allocation phase-out to 2034 are the observables; an extension that leaves packaging out would argue for the lower end of the range.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "environmental_r07": "Weight in the answer: The structural European energy-price disadvantage carries 0.07 of the 5.6 points, the 36th largest contribution of the 51 and 9 percent of the Environmental headwind. It reaches 32 percent of the pool, a European-manufacturing driver most present in laundry and auto dishwash, where powder towers and European plants are the most energy-intensive operations, and it carries the two Environmental penalties: the lowest pass-through of the six forces (6.3 percent) and an overlap correction of 27 percent shared with E-12 (the feedstock shock), E-01 (lauric oils) and E-03 (carbon costs).\n\nWhere it lands: 89 percent of the effect is European and fabric clean Europe carries 33 percent of it on its own (0.02 points); auto dishwash Europe carries 12 percent. Its category share is small, 2 percent of the auto dishwash contraction and 2 percent of fabric clean, because energy is a low single-digit share of conversion cost even where the gap is widest. It is the regional overlay to E-12: the feedstock shock is global, this driver is the European premium on top of it, and E-03 carries the carbon price on the same energy; the three should not be added as separate exposures.\n\nWhen it lands: Front-loaded to 2027, because the increment is dominated by the 2026 gas shock on top of a gap that already existed: 95 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027 and the whole of it by 2028.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range is narrow, 0.06 to 0.08 points; the gap is measured (EU industrial electricity at roughly double US prices in 2025, European gas at 6.2 times the US price in July 2026 after the Hormuz closure) and the gas path sets the size. The series to watch are the World Bank Pink Sheet gas series, the IEA and Eurostat industrial price series, and the Cefic closures radar, since 37 million tonnes or about 9 percent of European chemical capacity closed in 2022 to 2025 and further closures thin the European sources of surfactant intermediates and packaging resins; a European gas price back below three times the US level would argue for the lower end of the range.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "environmental_r12": "Weight in the answer: The 2026 feedstock, resin and freight shock carries 0.40 of the 5.6 points, the seventh largest contribution of the 51 and 49 percent of the Environmental force's headwind, half of that force on its own. It reaches 67 percent of the pool, one of the broadest reaches in the base, but the Environmental force has the lowest pass-through of the six (6.3 percent), and the overlap correction takes 24 percent, more than any driver outside the Technology and Environmental cost stacks, because it sits on the same cost cells as E-01 (lauric oils), E-07 (European energy) and E-03 (carbon costs); the four are one cost stack and the correction already discounts them together.\n\nWhere it lands: Fabric clean Europe (0.06 points) leads, then fabric clean North America (0.03 points) and hair colour Europe (0.03 points). The regional split is unusual for a European-led driver: 22 percent of the effect sits in High Growth, more than in North America (18 percent), because import-priced feedstock and freight weigh most there while North America buys domestic feedstock. By category the effect is spread thinly, 11 percent of the insect control contraction at most, which is the signature of a COGS driver: it hits every packaging- and surfactant-intensive line at a similar rate rather than one category hard.\n\nWhen it lands: Among the earliest-arriving drivers in the base, with E-01, E-07, G-08 and G-13: 93 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027 and 99 percent by 2028. It is modelled as a shock that is already observed, with the onset set so that the 2026 path year carries it; under ceteris paribus the cost increase is unrecovered, since any pricing recovery is a management response, which the simulation holds constant.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.30 to 0.46 points and it accounts for 4 percent of the portfolio band's variance. The width is the 2027 oil level: the World Bank path has Brent averaging USD 86 in 2026 and falling to USD 70 in 2027, and a second spike or a faster normalisation moves the persistence of the shock, not its existence. It moves with C-06 (the cost-of-living squeeze), which depends on the same path. The observables are Henkel's direct-material guidance (raised to a high single-digit increase for 2026 in May, confirmed in August), the ICIS building-block series and the China ethylene and paraxylene overcapacity that forms the post-2028 downside-price branch.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "government_r02": "Weight in the answer: The EU microplastics restriction carries 0.09 of the 5.6 points, the 30th largest contribution of the 51 and 7 percent of the Government headwind. It reaches 33 percent of the pool, laundry additives and finishers above all, with a European regional profile; its expected effect is small because the cost is a bounded reformulation, not a format decision.\n\nWhere it lands: 91 percent of the effect is European. Fabric clean Europe carries 26 percent of it (0.02 points), and its highest category shares are laundry additives (3 percent of the contraction) and fabric finishers (3 percent), where solid opacifiers and fragrance encapsulates are the exposures that remain once PVA film is excluded by the solubility clause. It shares the European laundry cells with G-04 (packaging) and G-16 (the Detergents Regulation) and the hair cells with G-03 and G-17; the overlap correction discounts them together. Its real interaction is with C-28 (scent-led premiumisation): the encapsulate gate of October 2029 lands on the scent-premium pool that C-28 expands, so the two should be read as one scent story with a regulatory cost inside it.\n\nWhen it lands: A staircase of three dated gates that a linear ramp matches: rinse-off cosmetics 17 October 2027, detergents 17 October 2028, leave-on cosmetics and encapsulated fragrances 17 October 2029. 71 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 94 percent by 2029 and the effect is complete by 2030.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range is one of the narrowest in the base, 0.08 to 0.10 points, because the cost is a bounded reformulation dated in law. What would move the read is not the regulation but the formulation position: Henkel states it has replaced synthetic encapsulates in Europe and is still seeking a fully biodegradable opacifier, so the opacifier for LHC liquids by October 2028 is the one open item. A delisting of a fragranced variant at a gate, or a claim withdrawal on long-lasting scent, would be the observable that the cost is larger than a reformulation.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "government_r03": "Weight in the answer: The EU cosmetics ingredient-restriction stack carries 0.17 of the 5.6 points, the 15th largest contribution of the 51 and 13 percent of the Government headwind. It is a hair-and-body driver of European law, so it reaches 26 percent of the pool: no laundry or home-care cell carries it (the detergent allergen rules sit in G-16), and outside Europe only the markets that follow the EU annexes with a lag carry a fraction of it.\n\nWhere it lands: Hair colour Europe carries 36 percent of the effect on its own (0.06 points, 12 percent of that cell's shift), because hair-dye intermediates and allergens are colour chemistry; hair care Europe (0.03 points) and hair styling Europe (0.02 points) follow, and Europe holds 75 percent of the total. It accounts for 11 percent of the hair colour contraction, its signature category, and 7 percent of hair styling. It shares the European hair cells with G-17 (the wastewater levy), G-04 (packaging) and G-02 (microplastics), which together form the Government headwind on European hair.\n\nWhen it lands: The gates are dated and the ramp is linear across them: Omnibus VII applicable 1 September 2025, Omnibus VIII in force 2 February 2026, the 56 fragrance allergens with no placing on the market after 31 July 2026 and no making available after 31 July 2028, PFHxA from 10 October 2026, D4, D5 and D6 in leave-on products from 6 June 2027. 71 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027 and 94 percent by 2028; the effect is complete by 2029, early in the horizon.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.15 to 0.19 points, the narrowest of the large regulatory drivers, because the reformulation cost per gate is well understood and the dates are in law. What could still move it is the universal REACH PFAS restriction, later and smaller than its headline suggests (RAC and SEAC opinions in 2026, a Commission decision not before Q3 2027, application in 2029 or later, and PFAS intentionally added in only 0.41 percent of registered cosmetic listings), and the final text of Chemicals Omnibus VI on the CMR exemption, where no relief is the working assumption. Those two decisions are the observables.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "government_r04": "Weight in the answer: The packaging and EPR cost stack carries 0.43 of the 5.6 points, the fifth largest contribution of the 51 and 32 percent of the Government force's headwind, the largest regulatory item in the base. Its expected effect is the largest of the Government drivers, but it reaches only 51 percent of the pool, because its exposure is deepest in laundry and toilet care and its regional profile is European, with the US state schemes a secondary presence and almost nothing in Asia and High Growth.\n\nWhere it lands: Fabric clean Europe alone carries 21 percent of the effect (0.09 points, 9 percent of that cell's shift), the highest single-cell concentration among the five largest drivers, because laundry bottles and multipack film are the biggest packaging tonnage in the portfolio. Hair colour Europe (0.05 points) and fabric clean North America (0.05 points) follow. Europe holds 72 percent of the effect and North America 21 percent. In the small categories it is a visible share of the whole: 11 percent of the toilet care contraction and 9 percent of hard-surface cleaner, the rim-block cages and trigger sprays. It shares the European laundry cells with G-16 (the Detergents Regulation) and G-02 (microplastics), and the three make up most of the Government headwind on European laundry.\n\nWhen it lands: A linear ramp across dated gates: UK pEPR invoicing since October 2025 and Oregon since July 2025 at the start, PPWR harmonised labels from 12 August 2028, recycled-content minima, recyclability grades and the Annex V bans from 1 January 2030, with eco-modulated fees about 18 months after the 2028 design-for-recycling acts. 64 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028 and 93 percent by 2030; the half-way point is 2028.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.32 to 0.50 points and it accounts for 5 percent of the portfolio band's variance. The dates are in law; the width is the fee level, since eco-modulated fees are still being set by the national schemes and could halve or double the cost. The signals to watch are the fee schedules as they are published (UK plastic GBP 423 per tonne for 2025/26 is the first datum), recyclate availability after the 2030 gates rather than today's weak recyclate price, and the PCR position of the European laundry bottles, already at 65 percent: a fee schedule that rewards that position would argue for the lower half of the range.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "government_r05": "Weight in the answer: The EmpCo greenwashing rules carry 0.08 of the 5.6 points, the 32nd largest contribution of the 51 and 6 percent of the Government headwind. The driver reaches 38 percent of the pool, laundry above all, because eco sub-ranges and generic claims are densest there, and its small expected effect reflects a bounded relabelling and substantiation cost; the Government pass-through and overlap correction are mid-range.\n\nWhere it lands: 83 percent of the effect is European and fabric clean Europe carries 28 percent of it (0.02 points). Its category share is small and even, 2 percent of the hard-surface cleaner contraction and about the same in fabric clean and toilet care. It shares the European laundry cells with G-04 (packaging), G-16 (the Detergents Regulation) and G-02 (microplastics), the European compliance stack the overlap correction discounts together. It also carries the substantiation cost that C-04 (clinical-efficacy premiumisation) relies on: proof-based claims are where the upside sits, generic claims are where this cost sits.\n\nWhen it lands: A step: Directive (EU) 2024/825 applies from 27 September 2026, so 76 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027 and the whole of it by 2028. Nothing is added later; the Green Claims Directive is politically dead (withdrawal announced 20 June 2025) and drives no parameter.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range is one of the narrowest in the base, 0.07 to 0.09 points, because the date is in law and the cost is bounded. What would move the read is enforcement practice rather than legislation, so the observables are the first national enforcement actions against generic claims (\"eco-friendly\", \"biodegradable\", \"climate friendly\") in laundry and cleaners, and whether certification-based labels replace them at a cost or with a delisting. A delisting of an eco sub-range would be the signal that the cost is larger than a relabelling; the mild upside against small eco challengers is already netted inside the score.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "government_r06": "Weight in the answer: The nature and supply-chain due-diligence stack carries 0.06 of the 5.6 points, the 42nd largest contribution of the 51 and 4 percent of the Government headwind. It reaches 33 percent of the pool, the surfactant-heavy categories where the pass-through from oleochemical suppliers lands, with a European regional profile because EUDR and CSDDD costs land on goods placed on the EU market; its expected effect is among the smallest in the base because the obligation bites on HCB's suppliers, not on HCB.\n\nWhere it lands: 82 percent of the effect is European; fabric clean Europe carries 26 percent of it (0.02 points) and hair care Europe 14 percent. Its largest category shares are hand dishwash (2 percent of the contraction) and hair care (2 percent), where surfactant intensity is highest. It shares the European cells with G-04 (packaging), G-16 (the Detergents Regulation) and G-02 (microplastics). The palm price premium that EUDR creates (USD 350 to 400 per tonne on crude palm kernel oil) is deliberately not here; it sits in E-01, and this driver carries only the due-diligence obligation itself: reference numbers under Regulation (EU) 2025/2650 and the CSDDD conduct duties.\n\nWhen it lands: Linear to 2030 from a dated start: EUDR applies from 30 December 2026 for large and medium operators, so 2027 is the first affected path year; CSDDD after Omnibus I applies from 26 July 2029 only above 5,000 employees and EUR 1.5bn turnover. 52 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 95 percent by 2030 and the effect is complete by 2031.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range is narrow, 0.04 to 0.07 points; the dates are in law and the width is the pass-through premium, which varies with supplier compliance. The scope has been narrowing rather than widening (surfactants under HS 3402 and finished detergents and shampoos are out of EUDR scope; civil liability and mandatory transition plans were removed from CSDDD), so the observable is the reverse of the usual: a widening of scope, or a supplier compliance failure in the palm kernel chain at the December 2026 application date, would be the trigger to re-score. The Kunming-Montreal framework and TNFD are not company mandates and add nothing here.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "government_r08": "Weight in the answer: The post-IEEPA US tariff regime carries 0.05 of the 5.6 points, the 44th largest contribution of the 51 and 4 percent of the Government headwind. It is a North American cost driver of imported inputs and finished goods with only a residual presence elsewhere, so it reaches 18 percent of the pool; the Government pass-through and overlap correction are mid-range, and its small expected effect reflects a regime that Henkel calls manageable and that P&G and Church & Dwight book as a 2026 tailwind.\n\nWhere it lands: North America holds 59 percent of the effect and Europe 27 percent although Europe carries the same residual presence as the growth markets, because a residual presence on the region that holds half the pool outweighs the same presence on a fifth of it; fabric clean North America carries 20 percent of it. Its largest category shares are hair styling (2 percent of the contraction), where aerosol cans fall under the Section 232 metals tariff at 50 percent, and body care (1 percent). It sits on the North American cells with G-15 (SNAP retrenchment), G-13 (MoCRA and the state bans) and G-04 (packaging), the North American Government stack.\n\nWhen it lands: Front-loaded to 2027 with a long tail: 93 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027 and the whole of it by 2029. The Supreme Court struck down the IEEPA tariffs on 20 February 2026, the Section 122 surcharge ran from 24 February to 24 July 2026 and the Section 301 tariffs of 10 or 12.5 percent on 60 trading partners were announced on 24 July 2026, so the regime the driver describes is already in place.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.03 to 0.07 points, the ordinary width for a driver of its size, and what sits inside it is policy volatility in both directions: the 2026 average effective rate of 6.6 percent is the highest since 1969, yet P&G booked a 40 basis point net tariff benefit to gross margin in its June 2026 quarter and Church & Dwight expects about USD 15m of refunds in H2 2026. The observables are the Tax Foundation's effective-rate tracker, the snap-back clauses in the EU regulation of 25 June 2026 that eliminated its duties on most US industrial goods, and the Section 301 investigation into German pharmaceutical pricing opened in June 2026; a re-escalation on the transatlantic route would argue for the upper end of the range, refunds landing as guided for the lower end.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "government_r13": "Weight in the answer: MoCRA and the US state ingredient bans carry 0.04 of the 5.6 points, the 48th largest contribution of the 51 and 3 percent of the Government headwind. It is a North America-only cosmetics driver, so it reaches 11 percent of the pool, the smallest reach of any regulatory driver, since the North American hair and body cells are small in the estimated mix; the Government pass-through and overlap correction are mid-range.\n\nWhere it lands: All of the effect is North American: body care North America carries 27 percent of it and hair colour North America 24 percent, where phenylenediamines and formaldehyde releasers are hair-colour chemistry; in hair colour North America it accounts for 11 percent of the cell's shift, a larger share of a small cell. It accounts for 2 percent of the body care contraction and 1 percent of hair colour. It sits on the North American cells with G-15 (SNAP retrenchment), G-04 (packaging) and G-08 (tariffs), and it is the North American twin of G-03, the EU ingredient stack, on the same colour chemistry.\n\nWhen it lands: Front-loaded to 2027 across enacted state dates (Washington from 2025 with 25 formaldehyde releasers from 2027, Colorado and Minnesota 2025, Vermont and Maine 2026, Oregon and New Mexico 2027): 95 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027 and the whole of it by 2028.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range is narrow, 0.03 to 0.04 points, because the state bans are enacted and dated and MoCRA's registration, listing and safety-substantiation duties are live. The federal layer has turned deregulatory (GMP only as draft guidance, no fragrance-allergen rule proposed, the talc testing rule withdrawn in November 2025, no PFAS ban proposed in December 2025), which caps the size. The signals to watch are new state enactments, in particular a PFAS ban in a large state, and any federal rule that moves from guidance to regulation; a federal GMP rule would be the one item that widens this driver.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "government_r15": "Weight in the answer: US low-income transfer retrenchment carries 0.08 of the 5.6 points, the 33rd largest contribution of the 51 and 6 percent of the Government headwind. It is a North America-only driver of the value tier, so it reaches 18 percent of the pool; the Government pass-through and overlap correction are mid-range, and its weight is set by the one region it touches.\n\nWhere it lands: All of the effect is North American and fabric clean North America carries 52 percent of it on its own (0.04 points, 7 percent of that cell's shift), with body care North America at 20 percent: laundry, bar soap and body wash are where HCB's value-weighted North American portfolio (all, Purex, Dial) over-indexes. It accounts for 4 percent of the body care contraction and 2 percent of fabric clean. It sits on the North American laundry cells with G-04 (packaging), G-08 (tariffs) and G-13 (MoCRA and the state bans) in its own force, and next to C-34 (household formation) and C-06 (the squeeze) in the Consumer force; the three demand-side items, this driver, C-34 and C-06's North American part, are one policy regime and should be read together.\n\nWhen it lands: Front-loaded to 2028: the work requirements have applied since 2 November 2025, participation had fallen to 37.4 million by March 2026, and the state cost-share from 1 October 2027 keeps the pressure on. 84 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027, 97 percent by 2028 and the effect is complete by 2029.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range is narrow, 0.06 to 0.09 points. The participation drop is measured, but the pass-through to non-food categories rests on a stated-intent survey (half of SNAP shoppers say they plan to buy lower-priced brands) and the FY2028 leg on state budgets. The observables are Circana's SNAP-household CPG spend series (down USD 4.6bn in Q1 2026, called the new baseline), US grocery units (down about 2 percent year on year) and the state cost-share decisions from October 2027; a measured trade-down in laundry and body wash at the scale of the stated intent would argue for the upper end of the range.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "government_r16": "Weight in the answer: The Detergents and Surfactants Regulation carries 0.18 of the 5.6 points, the 13th largest contribution of the 51 and 14 percent of the Government headwind. It reaches 28 percent of the pool: every laundry and home-care category is in scope and no hair category is, and it is European by law, with only Türkiye's alignment with EU chemicals law giving it a small High Growth presence. The Government pass-through and overlap correction are mid-range.\n\nWhere it lands: 94 percent of the effect is European and fabric clean Europe alone carries 39 percent of it (0.07 points), one of the highest single-cell concentrations in the base; auto dishwash Europe (0.03 points, 9 percent of that cell's shift) is next. Its category share is even across the home-care categories, 7 percent of the auto dishwash contraction and about the same in fabric care and hard-surface cleaner, which is what a per-SKU compliance cost looks like. It sits on the European laundry cells with G-04 (packaging and EPR) and G-02 (microplastics); the three are one European compliance stack on the same SKUs and the overlap correction discounts them together.\n\nWhen it lands: A linear ramp across dated gates: the CLP relabelling waves from 1 July 2026 and 1 January 2027 at the start, the digital product passport and unique identifier for every detergent placed on the EU market from 23 September 2029 at the end. 52 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 95 percent by 2030; the half-way point is 2028. The film biodegradability requirement of Article 4(3) bites by 23 March 2032, after the peak, and its criteria are still to be set by delegated act.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range is narrow, 0.15 to 0.21 points, because the application dates are in law. The width is the unquantified cost of the passport and of the film criteria, and the film criteria are the item that could change the read: Annex I Part B is still empty, and criteria that unit-dose films cannot meet would move this from a labelling cost to a format decision on the largest laundry formats. The delegated act on those criteria and the national CLP enforcement waves are the observables.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "government_r17": "Weight in the answer: The wastewater levy under Directive (EU) 2024/3019 carries 0.14 of the 5.6 points, the 20th largest contribution of the 51 and 10 percent of the Government headwind. It is a European-only driver on every cosmetic unit and on nothing else, so it reaches 22 percent of the pool, a narrower reach than two thirds of the base; its place in the ranking comes from one of the largest expected effects among the Government drivers, because the cost base to be funded runs into billions of euros a year across cosmetics and pharmaceuticals.\n\nWhere it lands: All of the effect is European and hair colour Europe carries 43 percent of it on its own (0.06 points, 11 percent of that cell's shift); hair care Europe carries 27 percent. It accounts for 8 percent of the hair colour contraction, 7 percent of hair styling and 6 percent of hair care. It shares the European hair cells with G-03 (the ingredient stack), G-04 (packaging) and G-02 (microplastics), and the overlap correction discounts the four together.\n\nWhen it lands: The latest-arriving of the regulatory drivers, and the one whose timing decides its weight: nothing before 2029, the first fee year, then a linear ramp with 31 percent of the terminal effect by 2030, 62 percent by 2032 and the half-way point only in 2032. Even at 2035 the expected materialisation is 95 percent, because the peak can slip past the horizon when the national schemes, due by 31 December 2028, are late. The fees fund quaternary-treatment capacity ahead of the 2033 gate (20 percent of large plants) rather than trailing it.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.08 to 0.19 points and it accounts for 3 percent of the portfolio band's variance, the ordinary width for a driver of its size, a little wider only because the peak can slip past the horizon; what sits inside it is two open items at once: the fee levels, unknown until the national schemes set them, and the exemptions for substances that are rapidly biodegradable in wastewater or generate no micropollutants, which are a formulation question. The direct legal challenges failed on 18 February 2026 (the General Court declared them inadmissible), so the remaining route is national-court referral. The signals to watch are the first national scheme designs and fee schedules and any referral from the Irish courts; a scheme design that exempts most rinse-off formulations would argue for the lower end of the range.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "technology_r01": "Weight in the answer: AI and automation productivity offsets 0.11 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the sixth largest tailwind of the 18 and 40 percent of the Technology force's tailwind. It reaches 58 percent of the pool, one of the broadest reaches in the base, because it is present in every category and in every region, and it is still a small item because the Technology pass-through is 6.9 percent and the overlap correction takes 28 percent, shared with T-13 (generative search), T-11 (shopping agents) and T-02 (bio-based substitution). A broad, thinly spread, heavily discounted driver is the profile of a productivity story whose savings are competed away.\n\nWhere it lands: Hair colour Europe carries 17 percent of the effect (0.02 points), because reformulation speed against the EU cosmetics gates is the one HCB-specific value; its largest share of any cell's shift is 8 percent, in hair colour Asia, and everything else is spread thinner. Europe holds 59 percent of the total and North America 26 percent, where adoption is led from. It offsets 4 percent of the hair colour contraction and little anywhere else.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2031, the pace of adoption rather than of realised savings: 53 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 96 percent by 2030, the half-way point is 2028 and the effect is complete by 2031. The timing is the least informative part of the read, because the driver is a net-of-reinvestment effect that no company has yet reported: the curve says when the tools are in use, not when a margin effect appears.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.06 to 0.17 points, wide relative to its size, because the net-of-reinvestment effect could be close to zero: adoption is documented (Unilever's claims-generation process 75 percent faster, digital twins with 10 percent capacity and 20 to 30 percent waste gains at site level, L'Oréal's formulation model trained on 1.8 million formulas), but the majors reinvest the savings (L'Oréal advertising at 32.6 percent of sales in H1 2026, Unilever brand investment stable at 16.1 percent), private-label suppliers get the same tools, and no company has disclosed a realised margin effect. The observable is exactly that disclosure: the first reported margin gain attributed to AI after reinvestment, at any of the majors, would justify re-scoring; until then the weight rests on adoption evidence alone.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "technology_r02": "Weight in the answer: Bio-based ingredient substitution offsets 0.06 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the 13th largest tailwind of the 18 and 21 percent of the Technology force's tailwind. It reaches 49 percent of the pool, a broad reach through laundry, dish and body, and the Technology force takes back most of it: the pass-through is 6.9 percent and the overlap correction 30 percent, among the heaviest in the base, shared with T-03 (compaction), T-11 (shopping agents) and T-13 (generative search) on the same laundry cells. The smallest expected effect in the base, shared with T-08, does the rest.\n\nWhere it lands: The two laundry-detergent cells carry 36 percent of the effect (fabric clean Europe 0.01 points, fabric clean North America 0.01 points), because enzymes are the one bio-based platform at scale and laundry is where they are used; Europe holds 61 percent of the total. No category carries more than 1 percent of its contraction offset from this driver. It is deliberately not the answer to E-01 (the lauric-oil squeeze): substitution away from lauric oils is a management response and does not offset E-01 inside the model, so the two should not be netted.\n\nWhen it lands: One of the later tailwinds, an s-curve to 2033 so that the biosurfactant and fermentation tail stays late: 25 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 77 percent by 2030, the half-way point is 2029 and completion only by 2033.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.02 to 0.09 points, from two fifths of the expected value to over half again above it, because only the enzyme leg is certain: Novonesis Household Care grew 8 percent organically in H1 2026 and the group raised its guidance, while no biosurfactant supplier claims cost parity with SLES or LAS and fermentation funding fell 44 percent to USD 357m in 2025 with high-profile closures. The signals to watch are Novonesis's household-care growth, any supplier claim of biosurfactant cost parity, and the funding series for fermentation; the first credible parity claim would justify re-scoring upward, and a further funding contraction would confirm the long tail.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "technology_r03": "Weight in the answer: Compaction and the dry-format shift offset 0.11 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the seventh largest tailwind of the 18 and 39 percent of the Technology force's tailwind. It reaches 34 percent of the pool, laundry above all, and the Technology force takes back 30 percent through the overlap correction it shares with T-11 (shopping agents), T-13 (generative search) and T-02 (bio-based substitution) on the same laundry cells, at a pass-through of 6.9 percent.\n\nWhere it lands: The two laundry-detergent cells carry 51 percent of the effect: fabric clean North America (0.03 points) and fabric clean Europe (0.03 points). North America holds 36 percent of the total, a high share for a tailwind, because that is where compaction and the premium dry format are moving fastest (Henkel North America's 16 percent dose concentration across all, Persil and Snuggle liquids; P&G's Tide evo tiles in stores across the US from April 2026). It offsets 4 percent of the fabric clean contraction and 3 percent of laundry additives.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2031 whose first years are already in the market: 53 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 96 percent by 2030, the half-way point is 2028 and the effect is complete by 2031. What would move the timing is retailer shelf decisions on the premium dry format: compaction inside existing liquids arrives at the pace of reformulation cycles, a new format shelved nationally arrives in one reset.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.07 to 0.14 points. The compaction increment could be half or double depending on retailer shelf decisions, and the driver carries its own capture risk: P&G owns the premium dry format in North America, so a larger category effect is not automatically a larger HCB effect. The observables are Tide evo's trajectory against P&G's internal projection of up to USD 500m in annual sales, the retailer resets that decide shelf space for tiles and pods, and the per-load premiums of 50 to 140 percent over liquids; a second premium dry format launched by another major would argue for the upper end of the range, a Tide evo that stays a niche for the lower end. Sheets are not part of the upside: they test poorly and stay sub-scale.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "technology_r08": "Weight in the answer: OEM auto-dosing and platform replenishment carry 0.04 of the 5.6 points, the 47th largest contribution of the 51 and 12 percent of the Technology headwind. Everything about the Technology force works against it: the pass-through is 6.9 percent and the overlap correction 31 percent, the heaviest in the base, shared with T-03 (compaction), T-11 (shopping agents) and T-13 (generative search) on the same laundry cells, and its expected effect is the smallest in the base, shared with T-02. It reaches 34 percent of the pool, laundry and auto dishwash above all.\n\nWhere it lands: Fabric clean Europe carries 32 percent of the effect and fabric clean North America 22 percent; Europe holds 64 percent of the total, the OEM auto-dosing epicentre (Miele TwinDos, Bosch i-DOS), and North America 29 percent, where Amazon Dash Replenishment and Scheduled Actions are the likelier route. Its category share is small everywhere, 1 percent of the fabric clean contraction at most. It is the appliance-side companion of T-11: agents intermediate the order there, the appliance or the platform intermediates it here, and the two should be read together as one replenishment-control story.\n\nWhen it lands: One of the latest drivers: nothing to speak of before 2028, 38 percent of the terminal effect by 2029, 68 percent by 2030, the half-way point in 2030 and completion only by 2034.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.02 to 0.06 points, from two fifths of the expected value to over half again above it, and the size and the onset both depend on OEM and platform strategies not yet taken; no public adoption data for auto-dosing appliances exist. HCB's own Persil Smartwash is a management response and cannot carry an expansion driver; as of September 2026 it is a limited Amazon launch. The signals to watch are the first published penetration figure for auto-dosing washers, Amazon's Dash Replenishment coverage across washers and dishwashers, and the European rollout of Alexa+, in early access in Germany, the UK, France, Italy, Spain and Austria as of July 2026; a penetration figure above 10 percent of European washer sales would justify re-scoring.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "technology_r11": "Weight in the answer: AI shopping agents carry 0.15 of the 5.6 points, the 17th largest contribution of the 51 and 48 percent of the Technology headwind, the largest item in that force. Its expected effect is the largest in the Technology force, yet the force treats it harshly: the pass-through is 6.9 percent and the overlap correction takes 30 percent, among the heaviest in the base, because it sits on the same laundry cells as T-03 (compaction), T-13 (generative search) and T-01 (AI productivity). It reaches 43 percent of the pool, the low-consideration replenishment categories.\n\nWhere it lands: Fabric clean North America carries 21 percent of the effect (0.03 points) and fabric clean Europe 14 percent; North America holds 39 percent of the total, almost as much as Europe, because retailer-owned agents (Amazon's Rufus in Alexa for Shopping, Google's Universal Cart) are a North American fact first. Its category share is small and even, 4 percent of the insect control contraction at most, across laundry, additives, auto dishwash and finishers. It is the transaction layer of a two-layer story whose discovery layer is T-13, and the two should be read together rather than added.\n\nWhen it lands: One of the later drivers: nothing in 2026, 25 percent of the terminal effect by 2028, 78 percent by 2030, the half-way point in 2029 and completion only by 2033. AI is under 1 percent of retailer traffic, so the s-curve starts from its flat foot.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.05 to 0.26 points, one of the widest relative to its size in the base, and it accounts for 6 percent of the portfolio band's variance, more than its rank suggests. The reason is that the sign itself is open: rating-led leaders such as Persil could gain from agents that reward highly rated products while mid-tier brands lose, and the onset depends on platform decisions not yet taken (OpenAI scaled Instant Checkout back in March 2026 because retailers would not cede checkout). The series to watch are AI's share of retailer traffic (Bain), Adobe's AI-referred traffic series, and whether an agent with default replenishment reaches scale in laundry or auto dishwash; the first published penetration figure for agent-initiated CPG orders would justify re-scoring in either direction.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    "technology_r13": "Weight in the answer: Generative search and AI answers carry 0.13 of the 5.6 points, the 22nd largest contribution of the 51 and 40 percent of the Technology headwind. It reaches 50 percent of the pool, a broad reach for a driver concentrated in hair, but the Technology force takes back most of it: the pass-through is 6.9 percent and the overlap correction 29 percent, because it shares its cells with T-01 (AI productivity), T-11 (shopping agents) and T-02 (bio-based substitution), and the base's four AI and digital drivers are already discounted as one cluster.\n\nWhere it lands: The effect is spread thinly: hair colour Europe (0.02 points) is the largest cell at 13 percent of the effect, and its largest share of any cell's shift is 8 percent, in hair colour Asia. Europe holds 53 percent and North America 30 percent. It accounts for 4 percent of the hair care contraction and 4 percent of hair colour, the beauty categories where AI-assisted discovery bites first. It is the discovery layer of a two-layer story whose transaction layer is T-11, and the two should be read together rather than added.\n\nWhen it lands: An early-peaking s-curve to 2029: 54 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027, 88 percent by 2028, the half-way point is 2027 and the effect is complete by 2030. The curve starts from the early flank of adoption, since AI referrals are under 1 percent of retail traffic.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.08 to 0.17 points and it accounts for 2 percent of the portfolio band's variance. The discovery shift is measured (Adobe's AI-referred traffic to US retail sites plus 693 percent in the 2025 holiday season and plus 138 percent in May 2026, converting 42 to 54 percent better than non-AI traffic; 38 percent of consumers in the UK, France and Germany using AI tools to research products), while the cost to brands of re-optimising is inferred, so the width is the cost side. The observables are the same Adobe and Bain series and the share of product pages that are not machine-readable, about a third today; a year in which AI referrals stay below 1 percent of retail traffic would argue for the lower end of the range.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
}
